1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,338
Here is a hospital database, one row per
patient.

2
00:00:03,338 --> 00:00:08,448
A researcher asks an innocent question: how
many patients have condition X?

3
00:00:08,448 --> 00:00:10,696
The hospital releases no records.

4
00:00:10,696 --> 00:00:12,331
It just says: forty-one.

5
00:00:12,567 --> 00:00:15,798
A week later, one new patient, Alice, is
admitted.

6
00:00:15,798 --> 00:00:17,478
The researcher asks again.

7
00:00:17,478 --> 00:00:19,287
The answer is now forty-two.

8
00:00:19,533 --> 00:00:24,534
Subtract, and the researcher has learned
something very specific about Alice, without

9
00:00:24,534 --> 00:00:25,887
seeing a single record.

10
00:00:25,887 --> 00:00:28,357
Before we go on, try to fix this yourself.

11
00:00:28,357 --> 00:00:32,828
Pause and ponder: would rounding the count
to the nearest ten protect Alice?

12
00:00:32,828 --> 00:00:35,064
Pause the video if you need more time.

13
00:00:45,300 --> 00:00:46,332
Most weeks, yes.

14
00:00:46,332 --> 00:00:51,621
But if the count goes from forty-four to
forty-five, the rounded answer jumps from

15
00:00:51,621 --> 00:00:54,394
forty to fifty, and Alice is exposed again.

16
00:00:54,394 --> 00:00:59,231
Any fixed rule that ever changes its answer
has a jump like that somewhere.

17
00:00:59,467 --> 00:01:02,867
So what would it even mean for a statistic
to be private?

18
00:01:02,867 --> 00:01:05,909
And if the fix is to add noise, how much is
enough?

19
00:01:06,150 --> 00:01:12,014
In 2006, Cynthia Dwork, Frank McSherry,
Kobbi Nissim and Adam Smith answered both

20
00:01:12,014 --> 00:01:18,023
questions in this paper: Calibrating Noise
to Sensitivity in Private Data Analysis.

21
00:01:18,023 --> 00:01:24,177
It founded what we now call differential
privacy, and in 2017 it won the Gödel Prize.

22
00:01:24,417 --> 00:01:29,401
This video covers its three big ideas: a
definition of privacy, a number called

23
00:01:29,401 --> 00:01:32,870
sensitivity, and a recipe for how much noise
is enough.

24
00:01:32,870 --> 00:01:37,791
Plus a surprising limit on what one
published table can achieve, if it must be

25
00:01:37,791 --> 00:01:39,179
private in this sense.

26
00:01:40,617 --> 00:01:43,518
First, a map of where this paper comes from.

27
00:01:43,518 --> 00:01:48,464
In 1965, Stanley Warner proposed randomized
response for sensitive surveys.

28
00:01:48,464 --> 00:01:51,695
In a popular version, you flip a coin in
private.

29
00:01:51,695 --> 00:01:53,607
Heads, you answer truthfully.

30
00:01:53,607 --> 00:01:57,431
Tails, you flip again and say yes for heads,
no for tails.

31
00:01:57,431 --> 00:02:02,970
Any single yes could be the coin talking,
yet over thousands of people the true rate

32
00:02:02,970 --> 00:02:04,487
can still be estimated.

33
00:02:04,487 --> 00:02:06,004
Keep this coin in mind.

34
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:11,588
Over the following decades, statisticians
and computer scientists refined such tricks,

35
00:02:11,588 --> 00:02:16,554
in two flavours: scramble the data going in,
or scramble the answers coming out.

36
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:21,118
Meanwhile, the obvious fix, just remove the
names, kept failing.

37
00:02:21,118 --> 00:02:26,382
Latanya Sweeney showed that ZIP code, birth
date and sex alone single out most

38
00:02:26,382 --> 00:02:32,387
Americans, and in 1997 she linked supposedly
anonymous hospital records to a public voter

39
00:02:32,387 --> 00:02:35,424
list and found the governor of
Massachusetts.

40
00:02:35,424 --> 00:02:41,092
The lesson: privacy must be a property of
the process, not of how the released table

41
00:02:41,092 --> 00:02:44,939
looks, and it must hold whatever else the
attacker knows.

42
00:02:45,184 --> 00:02:49,550
Then, in 2003, Irit Dinur and Kobbi Nissim
proved something sobering.

43
00:02:49,550 --> 00:02:54,738
Answer too many questions too accurately,
with errors much smaller than the square

44
00:02:54,738 --> 00:02:58,914
root of n, and an attacker can rebuild
almost the entire database.

45
00:02:58,914 --> 00:03:02,711
Our subtraction was the two-question version
of this attack.

46
00:03:02,711 --> 00:03:05,685
Noise is the price of answering many
questions.

47
00:03:05,934 --> 00:03:11,006
The hopeful flip side: with a limited number
of questions, modest noise is enough.

48
00:03:11,006 --> 00:03:16,202
Dwork, Nissim and colleagues built this into
a framework called SuLQ, for sub-linear

49
00:03:16,202 --> 00:03:19,047
queries, which answers noisy sums like
counts.

50
00:03:19,047 --> 00:03:21,089
Remember that limit on questions.

51
00:03:21,089 --> 00:03:23,192
This paper turns it into a budget.

52
00:03:23,434 --> 00:03:28,107
But SuLQ only covered sums, and its
definition tolerated a tiny chance of a

53
00:03:28,107 --> 00:03:28,792
large leak.

54
00:03:28,792 --> 00:03:33,902
This paper takes the leap: any function of
the data, one clean definition, and one

55
00:03:33,902 --> 00:03:35,522
simple rule for the noise.

56
00:03:35,522 --> 00:03:40,569
Fittingly, it appeared at a cryptography
conference, where the habit is to define

57
00:03:40,569 --> 00:03:44,682
security against every possible attacker
first, and then prove it.

58
00:03:47,033 --> 00:03:48,379
Here is the setting.

59
00:03:48,379 --> 00:03:53,359
A trusted server, the curator, holds a
database x: n rows, one per person.

60
00:03:53,359 --> 00:03:59,013
An analyst sends a query f, any function of
the database that returns a number, or a

61
00:03:59,013 --> 00:04:00,089
list of numbers.

62
00:04:00,333 --> 00:04:03,791
The curator never releases the honest
answer, f of x.

63
00:04:03,791 --> 00:04:07,770
Instead it draws random noise, Y, and
releases f of x plus Y.

64
00:04:07,770 --> 00:04:12,728
Because the analyst can keep asking, this is
called the interactive setting.

65
00:04:12,728 --> 00:04:18,274
The alternative, publishing one sanitized
table and walking away, is non-interactive.

66
00:04:18,274 --> 00:04:22,840
At the end, the paper proves the first is
fundamentally more powerful.

67
00:04:23,083 --> 00:04:27,558
So everything hinges on one question: what
distribution should Y have?

68
00:04:27,558 --> 00:04:30,050
Too little noise, and Alice is exposed.

69
00:04:30,050 --> 00:04:32,351
Too much, and the answer is useless.

70
00:04:32,351 --> 00:04:36,634
The title gives the answer: calibrate the
noise to the sensitivity.

71
00:04:36,634 --> 00:04:39,382
But first, what does private actually mean?

72
00:04:40,717 --> 00:04:43,225
The key move is to imagine two worlds.

73
00:04:43,225 --> 00:04:44,941
In one, the database is x.

74
00:04:44,941 --> 00:04:48,770
In the other, it is x prime: identical
except for one row.

75
00:04:48,770 --> 00:04:49,628
Say, Alice's.

76
00:04:49,628 --> 00:04:52,334
Databases like this are called neighbors.

77
00:04:52,334 --> 00:04:57,483
In the hospital, Alice was added; here, her
row just says something different.

78
00:04:57,483 --> 00:05:01,048
Either way, no single person's row should
matter much.

79
00:05:01,284 --> 00:05:06,068
Run the mechanism, the curator's randomized
answering rule, in each world.

80
00:05:06,068 --> 00:05:11,434
Whatever the analyst gets to see, the paper
calls the transcript; for now, a single

81
00:05:11,434 --> 00:05:12,275
noisy answer.

82
00:05:12,275 --> 00:05:17,576
Because the mechanism is random, each world
gives a whole distribution of possible

83
00:05:17,576 --> 00:05:18,094
outputs.

84
00:05:18,334 --> 00:05:22,790
Pick any output t, and compare the heights
of the two curves there.

85
00:05:22,790 --> 00:05:25,185
Slide t along and watch their ratio.

86
00:05:25,185 --> 00:05:30,639
A private mechanism keeps that ratio close
to one everywhere: never above e to the

87
00:05:30,639 --> 00:05:33,566
epsilon, never below e to the minus epsilon.

88
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:38,945
That is the paper's definition, which it
calls epsilon-indistinguishability.

89
00:05:38,945 --> 00:05:42,059
The log of the ratio is the privacy loss at
t.

90
00:05:42,059 --> 00:05:47,948
For every pair of neighbors, every analyst
and every output, it must be at most epsilon

91
00:05:47,948 --> 00:05:49,167
in absolute value.

92
00:05:49,167 --> 00:05:54,785
The absolute value makes it symmetric: a
ratio of two and a ratio of one half count

93
00:05:54,785 --> 00:05:55,395
the same.

94
00:05:55,395 --> 00:05:59,592
For small epsilon, e to the epsilon is about
one plus epsilon.

95
00:05:59,834 --> 00:06:02,670
Let's test it on Warner's coin from our map.

96
00:06:02,670 --> 00:06:07,698
Pause and ponder: if Alice's true answer is
yes, how likely is she to say yes?

97
00:06:07,698 --> 00:06:08,730
And if it is no?

98
00:06:21,567 --> 00:06:26,906
If the truth is yes, she says yes three
quarters of the time: heads, or tails then

99
00:06:26,906 --> 00:06:27,296
heads.

100
00:06:27,296 --> 00:06:31,138
If it is no, only the second coin can say
yes: one quarter.

101
00:06:31,138 --> 00:06:36,412
The worst ratio is three, so the coin
satisfies the definition with epsilon equal

102
00:06:36,412 --> 00:06:39,082
to the log of three, about one point one.

103
00:06:39,317 --> 00:06:42,922
Now be the attacker, trying to tell the two
worlds apart.

104
00:06:42,922 --> 00:06:48,362
By Bayes' rule, your new odds are your old
odds times exactly this ratio, so they move

105
00:06:48,362 --> 00:06:52,536
by at most a factor of e to the epsilon,
whatever you knew before.

106
00:06:52,536 --> 00:06:57,850
Starting from fifty-fifty, epsilon one tenth
leaves you at most fifty-two and a half

107
00:06:57,850 --> 00:07:01,012
percent sure; epsilon one, at most seventy-
three.

108
00:07:01,012 --> 00:07:04,618
Epsilon is set by policy; the paper calls it
the leakage.

109
00:07:04,867 --> 00:07:06,918
Notice what this does not promise.

110
00:07:06,918 --> 00:07:11,984
If a study reveals that smokers get more
heart disease, and Alice smokes, people may

111
00:07:11,984 --> 00:07:13,915
now think she is at higher risk.

112
00:07:13,915 --> 00:07:19,102
That is not a privacy violation: the same
lesson could be learned from everyone else's

113
00:07:19,102 --> 00:07:23,747
data, so it would happen even if Alice's row
were replaced by someone else's.

114
00:07:25,183 --> 00:07:28,212
Why demand a ratio bound for every single
output?

115
00:07:28,212 --> 00:07:33,403
Cryptography often settles for something
weaker: statistical distance, half the area

116
00:07:33,403 --> 00:07:34,825
between the two curves.

117
00:07:34,825 --> 00:07:39,893
Equivalently, the most the probability of
any event can differ between the worlds.

118
00:07:39,893 --> 00:07:42,612
The paper shows why that is not enough here.

119
00:07:42,850 --> 00:07:47,821
Consider this mechanism: pick one row at
random and publish it, word for word.

120
00:07:47,821 --> 00:07:52,792
Change one person's row, and the output
distribution moves by only one over n.

121
00:07:52,792 --> 00:07:57,826
For a big database that is tiny, so by the
averaged measure this looks private.

122
00:07:58,066 --> 00:08:01,269
But every output is somebody's complete
record.

123
00:08:01,269 --> 00:08:06,720
The ratio test catches it: the output
showing Alice's real value has probability

124
00:08:06,720 --> 00:08:09,922
one over n in one world, and zero in the
other.

125
00:08:09,922 --> 00:08:11,421
The ratio is infinite.

126
00:08:11,666 --> 00:08:17,206
Averages let catastrophes slip through, as
long as each is rare for any single person.

127
00:08:17,206 --> 00:08:19,783
A ratio bound rules them out everywhere.

128
00:08:19,783 --> 00:08:25,194
It also settles our rounding question: at
the jump, one world gives that answer with

129
00:08:25,194 --> 00:08:28,350
probability one, the other with probability
zero.

130
00:08:28,350 --> 00:08:31,442
To pass this test, a mechanism has to be
random.

131
00:08:32,883 --> 00:08:34,769
So how much noise do we need?

132
00:08:34,769 --> 00:08:39,712
It depends on a single property of the
query: its sensitivity, the most that

133
00:08:39,712 --> 00:08:45,240
changing one row can ever change the answer,
over all pairs of neighboring databases,

134
00:08:45,240 --> 00:08:47,646
including ones that do not exist yet.

135
00:08:47,883 --> 00:08:53,431
For a counting query, like our hospital
count, one row changes the answer by at most

136
00:08:53,431 --> 00:08:53,696
one.

137
00:08:53,696 --> 00:08:54,752
Sensitivity one.

138
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:00,478
Now a histogram: split the possible values
of a row into d bins, and release how many

139
00:09:00,478 --> 00:09:01,638
rows fall in each.

140
00:09:01,638 --> 00:09:06,923
Before I show you, pause: if Alice's row
changes, how much can the whole histogram

141
00:09:06,923 --> 00:09:07,954
change in total?

142
00:09:07,954 --> 00:09:10,338
Does it depend on the number of bins?

143
00:09:19,183 --> 00:09:24,773
She moves out of one bin and into another:
one count goes down by one, another goes up

144
00:09:24,773 --> 00:09:25,228
by one.

145
00:09:25,228 --> 00:09:26,397
Total change: two.

146
00:09:26,397 --> 00:09:31,272
Adding up absolute changes across
coordinates like this is the L1 norm, the

147
00:09:31,272 --> 00:09:34,911
paper's way to measure sensitivity for lists
of numbers.

148
00:09:34,911 --> 00:09:38,746
And it is two whether there are five bins or
five thousand.

149
00:09:38,746 --> 00:09:41,540
It does not depend on the dimension at all.

150
00:09:41,783 --> 00:09:45,612
Contrast that with: what is the largest
income in the database?

151
00:09:45,612 --> 00:09:50,718
One billionaire can move that answer by a
billion, and with no cap there is no limit

152
00:09:50,718 --> 00:09:51,143
at all.

153
00:09:51,143 --> 00:09:55,033
The standard fix is to cap every value, say
at a million, first.

154
00:09:55,033 --> 00:09:59,895
Remember that trick: it is exactly what
private deep learning does to gradients.

155
00:10:00,133 --> 00:10:01,753
Two things to remember.

156
00:10:01,753 --> 00:10:07,669
Sensitivity is a property of the function
alone, not of the particular database, and

157
00:10:07,669 --> 00:10:09,570
it is not chosen by policy.

158
00:10:09,570 --> 00:10:10,979
Epsilon is a choice.

159
00:10:10,979 --> 00:10:12,528
Sensitivity is a fact.

160
00:10:13,967 --> 00:10:15,111
Now for the noise.

161
00:10:15,111 --> 00:10:20,386
The paper uses the Laplace distribution:
symmetric, sharply peaked, and falling off

162
00:10:20,386 --> 00:10:23,182
exponentially with distance from the center.

163
00:10:23,182 --> 00:10:26,106
Its width is set by a scale parameter,
lambda.

164
00:10:27,150 --> 00:10:28,398
Here is the trick.

165
00:10:28,398 --> 00:10:34,430
Center one Laplace curve at f of x, forty-
one, and another at f of x prime, forty-two:

166
00:10:34,430 --> 00:10:37,412
the output distributions of our two worlds.

167
00:10:37,650 --> 00:10:40,975
Now plot the log of their ratio for every
output t.

168
00:10:40,975 --> 00:10:44,170
To the left of forty-one, it is exactly
constant.

169
00:10:44,170 --> 00:10:46,909
To the right of forty-two, constant again.

170
00:10:46,909 --> 00:10:48,734
In between, a straight ramp.

171
00:10:48,734 --> 00:10:54,342
It never rises above one over lambda, and
never falls below minus one over lambda: the

172
00:10:54,342 --> 00:10:56,168
shift, divided by the scale.

173
00:10:56,817 --> 00:10:57,081
Why?

174
00:10:57,081 --> 00:11:02,575
On a log scale, a Laplace density is a tent
whose sides have slope one over lambda,

175
00:11:02,575 --> 00:11:03,502
never steeper.

176
00:11:03,502 --> 00:11:09,326
Slide the tent over, and the gap between the
tents can never exceed the slide times that

177
00:11:09,326 --> 00:11:09,723
slope.

178
00:11:09,723 --> 00:11:15,151
That is the whole design principle: privacy
needs noise whose log density is never

179
00:11:15,151 --> 00:11:15,548
steep.

180
00:11:16,284 --> 00:11:21,878
In symbols: two distances to t can differ by
at most the distance between the centers.

181
00:11:21,878 --> 00:11:27,016
So the privacy loss of any output is at most
the sensitivity divided by lambda.

182
00:11:27,016 --> 00:11:32,740
Set lambda to the sensitivity over epsilon,
and the ratio stays within e to the epsilon,

183
00:11:32,740 --> 00:11:35,667
for every output and every pair of
neighbors.

184
00:11:35,667 --> 00:11:37,489
Privacy, proven in one line.

185
00:11:37,734 --> 00:11:43,052
If the query returns several numbers, add
independent Laplace noise to each.

186
00:11:43,052 --> 00:11:48,861
The joint density then depends on the L1
distance, which is exactly why sensitivity

187
00:11:48,861 --> 00:11:50,750
is measured in the L1 norm.

188
00:11:50,750 --> 00:11:55,929
That is Proposition 1: Laplace noise of
scale S of f over epsilon in every

189
00:11:55,929 --> 00:11:56,699
coordinate.

190
00:11:56,934 --> 00:12:02,331
Pause and ponder: why not something simpler,
like uniform noise anywhere between minus

191
00:12:02,331 --> 00:12:03,398
ten and plus ten?

192
00:12:14,234 --> 00:12:15,407
Look at the edges.

193
00:12:15,407 --> 00:12:20,689
An output of fifty-one and a half is
possible if the true count is forty-two, but

194
00:12:20,689 --> 00:12:25,841
impossible if it is forty-one: the same
infinite ratio that sank the random-row

195
00:12:25,841 --> 00:12:26,493
mechanism.

196
00:12:26,493 --> 00:12:29,167
Good noise must never rule an output out.

197
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:31,633
And why not the familiar bell curve?

198
00:12:31,633 --> 00:12:36,843
On a log scale a Gaussian is a parabola,
steeper and steeper, so the gap between two

199
00:12:36,843 --> 00:12:39,200
shifted parabolas grows without bound.

200
00:12:39,200 --> 00:12:42,984
In the tails the ratio explodes, and no
single epsilon works.

201
00:12:42,984 --> 00:12:48,381
Hold on to that Gaussian, though: it comes
back, and it is the noise you would actually

202
00:12:48,381 --> 00:12:50,862
use to train a neural network privately.

203
00:12:51,100 --> 00:12:57,110
So here is the recipe in the title: noise
scale equals sensitivity divided by epsilon.

204
00:12:57,110 --> 00:13:00,115
A more sensitive question needs more noise.

205
00:13:00,115 --> 00:13:02,492
Stronger privacy needs more noise.

206
00:13:04,033 --> 00:13:08,482
Back to the hospital, with epsilon one half:
Laplace noise of scale two.

207
00:13:08,482 --> 00:13:12,252
Week one, the curator reports about forty-
three point seven.

208
00:13:12,252 --> 00:13:16,330
Week two, when the true count is forty-two,
about forty point six.

209
00:13:16,330 --> 00:13:20,717
Subtract as before, and Alice seems to have
lowered the count by three.

210
00:13:20,717 --> 00:13:25,537
A fifty-fifty guess about her can now move
to at most about sixty-two percent.

211
00:13:25,783 --> 00:13:30,349
Now look at what the noise scale depends on:
the sensitivity and epsilon.

212
00:13:30,349 --> 00:13:32,162
Not the size of the database.

213
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:38,178
With a hundred patients, noise of typical
size two is a few percent of the answer.

214
00:13:38,178 --> 00:13:40,640
With a million, it is a rounding error.

215
00:13:40,640 --> 00:13:45,943
Alice is protected by noise that is large
compared to her own contribution, which is

216
00:13:45,943 --> 00:13:48,658
one, but tiny compared to the population's.

217
00:13:49,900 --> 00:13:51,711
Privacy for individuals.

218
00:13:51,711 --> 00:13:53,597
Accuracy for populations.

219
00:13:53,597 --> 00:13:55,105
That is the bargain.

220
00:13:55,350 --> 00:13:57,575
Here is one you can answer yourself.

221
00:13:57,575 --> 00:14:01,283
Pause and ponder: the noise has size about
one over epsilon.

222
00:14:01,283 --> 00:14:04,928
What goes wrong if epsilon is much smaller
than one over n?

223
00:14:17,766 --> 00:14:23,474
The noise outgrows n, bigger than the count
could ever be, and the answer is pure noise.

224
00:14:23,474 --> 00:14:25,290
This is not Laplace's fault.

225
00:14:25,290 --> 00:14:30,869
Any two databases are linked by a chain of
at most n single-row changes, each changing

226
00:14:30,869 --> 00:14:36,252
probabilities by at most e to the epsilon,
so the ends differ by at most e to the n

227
00:14:36,252 --> 00:14:36,771
epsilon.

228
00:14:36,771 --> 00:14:41,766
If n epsilon is tiny, every database looks
alike, and nothing can be learned.

229
00:14:41,766 --> 00:14:46,630
This chain trick is called a hybrid
argument, and it comes back at the end.

230
00:14:48,067 --> 00:14:50,024
One question is never enough.

231
00:14:50,024 --> 00:14:55,220
Real analysts ask many, adaptively, each
chosen after seeing earlier answers.

232
00:14:55,220 --> 00:14:58,392
Maybe you spot a spike in one bin, and zoom
in.

233
00:14:58,634 --> 00:15:00,879
The paper's Theorem 1 handles this.

234
00:15:00,879 --> 00:15:05,563
Write the probability of the whole
transcript as a product, step by step.

235
00:15:05,563 --> 00:15:11,210
The analyst's choice of the next question is
the same in both worlds, so in the ratio it

236
00:15:11,210 --> 00:15:14,161
cancels, leaving one Laplace ratio per
answer.

237
00:15:14,161 --> 00:15:19,486
If you know reinforcement learning, this is
the trajectory-ratio trick: whatever is

238
00:15:19,486 --> 00:15:23,914
identical in both worlds cancels, and the
per-step log ratios add up.

239
00:15:24,150 --> 00:15:27,995
So privacy losses add up, and epsilon
behaves like a budget.

240
00:15:27,995 --> 00:15:32,290
Each answer spends part of it; once it is
spent, the curator stops.

241
00:15:32,290 --> 00:15:37,930
That is the answer to Dinur and Nissim: the
budget makes the limit on questions explicit

242
00:15:37,930 --> 00:15:38,892
and measurable.

243
00:15:40,434 --> 00:15:43,250
You can now compute a real saving yourself.

244
00:15:43,250 --> 00:15:46,722
Pause: a histogram with d bins, total budget
epsilon.

245
00:15:46,722 --> 00:15:51,242
Treat each bin as its own counting query and
split the budget evenly.

246
00:15:51,242 --> 00:15:53,404
How much noise does each bin get?

247
00:16:06,250 --> 00:16:10,956
Each bin gets one over d of the budget, so
noise of scale d over epsilon.

248
00:16:10,956 --> 00:16:16,436
Treated as one query with sensitivity two,
each bin gets two over epsilon, whatever d

249
00:16:16,436 --> 00:16:16,630
is.

250
00:16:16,630 --> 00:16:22,368
The earlier framework's sharper analysis got
this down to about the square root of d, but

251
00:16:22,368 --> 00:16:23,722
it still grew with d.

252
00:16:25,067 --> 00:16:27,558
Sensitivity reaches far beyond counts.

253
00:16:27,558 --> 00:16:29,526
Three examples from the paper.

254
00:16:29,834 --> 00:16:34,954
First, picture a social network where each
possible link is one row of the database.

255
00:16:34,954 --> 00:16:38,368
How many links must you cut to split the
network in two?

256
00:16:38,368 --> 00:16:43,489
That minimum cut moves by at most one when
one link changes, so it is one-sensitive.

257
00:16:43,489 --> 00:16:48,609
In general, any question of the form, how
many rows would you have to change to make

258
00:16:48,609 --> 00:16:50,804
something true, has sensitivity one.

259
00:16:51,050 --> 00:16:57,020
Second: if an algorithm that rarely looks at
any particular row, like one working from a

260
00:16:57,020 --> 00:17:02,312
small random sample, approximates f to
within sigma most of the time, on every

261
00:17:02,312 --> 00:17:05,772
database, then f has sensitivity at most two
sigma.

262
00:17:05,772 --> 00:17:06,858
That is Lemma 1.

263
00:17:07,100 --> 00:17:12,321
Third, the answer need not be a number: a
ranking, a set, a string of bits, anything

264
00:17:12,321 --> 00:17:14,310
with a distance between answers.

265
00:17:14,310 --> 00:17:19,531
Pick an output with probability that decays
exponentially with its distance from the

266
00:17:19,531 --> 00:17:23,323
true answer, at a rate of epsilon over twice
the sensitivity.

267
00:17:23,323 --> 00:17:28,171
For bit strings, that flips each bit with
probability a little below one half:

268
00:17:28,171 --> 00:17:30,844
Warner's coin again, applied to the answer.

269
00:17:33,683 --> 00:17:35,661
Now the last part of the paper.

270
00:17:35,661 --> 00:17:41,148
Statisticians and data miners traditionally
prefer the non-interactive model: sanitize

271
00:17:41,148 --> 00:17:44,912
the data once, publish it, and let anyone
compute anything.

272
00:17:44,912 --> 00:17:47,208
Can that work under this definition?

273
00:17:47,450 --> 00:17:49,643
The paper proves a striking limit.

274
00:17:49,643 --> 00:17:51,900
Let each row be a string of d bits.

275
00:17:51,900 --> 00:17:57,574
Here is a family of simple counting queries:
give each row its own mask, a subset of bit

276
00:17:57,574 --> 00:18:02,410
positions, and count the rows with an odd
number of ones inside their mask.

277
00:18:02,410 --> 00:18:07,504
Each query has sensitivity one, so an
interactive curator can answer any one of

278
00:18:07,504 --> 00:18:10,212
them with noise of about one over epsilon.

279
00:18:10,450 --> 00:18:14,171
But a one-shot release must prepare for all
of them at once.

280
00:18:14,171 --> 00:18:19,566
Theorem 3 shows that for at least two thirds
of these queries, the release looks almost

281
00:18:19,566 --> 00:18:24,775
the same for a random database where every
row has even parity, true answer zero, as

282
00:18:24,775 --> 00:18:27,628
for one where every row is odd, true answer
n.

283
00:18:27,628 --> 00:18:32,775
The most extreme difference possible, and it
cannot be seen, unless the database is

284
00:18:32,775 --> 00:18:37,736
exponentially large: roughly, every four
extra bits per row doubles the rows you

285
00:18:37,736 --> 00:18:38,418
would need.

286
00:18:38,666 --> 00:18:43,755
Pause and ponder: couldn't an analyst just
ask the interactive curator all of these

287
00:18:43,755 --> 00:18:44,490
queries too?

288
00:18:55,333 --> 00:18:57,098
No: the budget would run out.

289
00:18:57,098 --> 00:19:01,844
The curator only has to answer the few
questions someone actually asks, chosen

290
00:19:01,844 --> 00:19:02,209
later.

291
00:19:02,209 --> 00:19:07,320
A published release cannot know which few
those will be, so it must be ready for all

292
00:19:07,320 --> 00:19:08,294
of them at once.

293
00:19:08,294 --> 00:19:09,754
That is where it breaks.

294
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:11,937
The proof idea uses two facts.

295
00:19:11,937 --> 00:19:16,328
Privacy forces the release to treat every
possible row almost alike.

296
00:19:16,328 --> 00:19:21,752
And a random mask splits all rows into two
halves mixed like salt and pepper, so the

297
00:19:21,752 --> 00:19:24,722
even half is like a random poll of
everything.

298
00:19:24,722 --> 00:19:30,404
Then the chain trick: start from completely
random rows, and swap them one at a time for

299
00:19:30,404 --> 00:19:31,050
even ones.

300
00:19:31,050 --> 00:19:36,022
Each swap barely moves the output, and
unless n is huge, all n swaps together

301
00:19:36,022 --> 00:19:36,991
barely move it.

302
00:19:36,991 --> 00:19:41,381
The same goes for odd rows, so both
databases look like random rows.

303
00:19:41,616 --> 00:19:47,193
Warner's coin, randomized response, where
each person scrambles their own row so

304
00:19:47,193 --> 00:19:50,538
nobody holds the raw data, is even more
limited.

305
00:19:50,538 --> 00:19:56,672
Proposition 2: even when every row uses the
same mask, for most masks it cannot estimate

306
00:19:56,672 --> 00:19:59,878
the odd count unless n is exponentially
large.

307
00:20:00,116 --> 00:20:01,960
Careful with the quantifiers.

308
00:20:01,960 --> 00:20:06,729
For any one query known in advance, a one-
shot release can answer it well.

309
00:20:06,729 --> 00:20:12,197
What is impossible, unless the database is
exponentially large, is one private release

310
00:20:12,197 --> 00:20:13,850
that answers most of them.

311
00:20:13,850 --> 00:20:19,254
The lesson: for broad, flexible accuracy
with strong privacy, keep the curator in the

312
00:20:19,254 --> 00:20:19,572
loop.

313
00:20:21,217 --> 00:20:24,453
Back to our map, to see what grew from this
paper.

314
00:20:24,453 --> 00:20:29,371
The same year, in an invited paper titled
simply Differential Privacy, Dwork

315
00:20:29,371 --> 00:20:32,025
introduced the name the field still uses.

316
00:20:32,025 --> 00:20:37,008
Another 2006 paper, with Kenthapadi,
McSherry, Mironov and Naor, added a tiny

317
00:20:37,008 --> 00:20:37,849
slack, delta.

318
00:20:37,849 --> 00:20:41,991
Remember the Gaussian whose ratio escaped
the band in the tails?

319
00:20:41,991 --> 00:20:46,198
Delta pays for those rare tails, and lets
the bell curve back in.

320
00:20:46,434 --> 00:20:51,906
Scoring every possible answer became
McSherry and Talwar's exponential mechanism.

321
00:20:51,906 --> 00:20:57,377
The privacy budget grew into a theory of
composition, with a surprise from Dwork,

322
00:20:57,377 --> 00:21:03,322
Rothblum and Vadhan: allow that tiny delta,
and the total loss of k questions grows only

323
00:21:03,322 --> 00:21:05,079
like the square root of k.

324
00:21:05,317 --> 00:21:11,010
Warner's coin, the model Section four showed
is most limited, is what we now call local

325
00:21:11,010 --> 00:21:15,657
differential privacy, used by Google in
Chrome and by Apple on iPhones.

326
00:21:15,657 --> 00:21:17,096
Why the weakest model?

327
00:21:17,096 --> 00:21:22,594
Nobody has to be trusted with the raw data,
and with millions of users, a few simple

328
00:21:22,594 --> 00:21:24,688
statistics can afford the noise.

329
00:21:24,934 --> 00:21:30,114
In 2016, Abadi and colleagues made it
practical to train deep networks privately:

330
00:21:30,114 --> 00:21:35,229
clip each example's gradient, like our
income cap, which bounds its sensitivity;

331
00:21:35,229 --> 00:21:39,386
add Gaussian noise; and track the budget
over thousands of steps.

332
00:21:39,386 --> 00:21:43,351
If you train models on patient data, this is
your entry point.

333
00:21:43,351 --> 00:21:48,723
A trained network is just another f of x,
and attacks that ask whether a patient was

334
00:21:48,723 --> 00:21:52,432
in the training set are our subtraction
attack, scaled up.

335
00:21:52,667 --> 00:21:58,146
And for the 2020 census, the US Census
Bureau protected its published tables with

336
00:21:58,146 --> 00:22:03,287
differential privacy; only a few counts,
like state populations, were exact.

337
00:22:03,287 --> 00:22:05,384
But that is a one-shot release.

338
00:22:05,384 --> 00:22:08,496
Pause and ponder: does Section four forbid
it?

339
00:22:17,334 --> 00:22:17,536
No.

340
00:22:17,536 --> 00:22:22,463
The Census tuned its release for a fixed set
of tables chosen in advance.

341
00:22:22,463 --> 00:22:28,200
Section four only rules out one release that
is accurate for most of a huge family of

342
00:22:28,200 --> 00:22:28,875
questions.

343
00:22:32,217 --> 00:22:33,034
Let's recap.

344
00:22:33,034 --> 00:22:39,097
One: privacy means changing any one person's
row changes the probability of any output by

345
00:22:39,097 --> 00:22:41,618
at most a factor of e to the epsilon.

346
00:22:41,618 --> 00:22:46,318
Two: a query's sensitivity is the most one
row can change its answer.

347
00:22:46,318 --> 00:22:52,109
Three: Laplace noise with scale sensitivity
over epsilon makes it private, with error

348
00:22:52,109 --> 00:22:54,629
that does not grow with the database.

349
00:22:54,629 --> 00:23:00,352
Four: one private published table cannot
answer most simple parity counts unless the

350
00:23:00,352 --> 00:23:02,532
database is exponentially large.

351
00:23:03,167 --> 00:23:06,276
Some questions to test yourself; pause after
each.

352
00:23:06,276 --> 00:23:11,252
First: what is the sensitivity of the
average of n numbers between zero and one?

353
00:23:19,500 --> 00:23:24,919
Second: in a medical imaging dataset, each
patient contributes fifty slices, and each

354
00:23:24,919 --> 00:23:27,342
slice's gradient is clipped to size C.

355
00:23:27,342 --> 00:23:30,658
How much can one patient change the summed
gradient?

356
00:23:40,900 --> 00:23:44,644
Third: why does the median give the Laplace
mechanism trouble?

357
00:23:44,644 --> 00:23:48,026
Answers, and more exercises, are in the
companion notes.

358
00:23:56,267 --> 00:24:01,203
Next time someone says a dataset is safe
because it is anonymized, you will know the

359
00:24:01,203 --> 00:24:05,611
better questions: what is the epsilon, and
what counts as one person's row?

